---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Specimen from "../components/Specimen.astro";
---

<Layout title="Change the way the world reads — a Lexend type sample">
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 py-20 sm:py-28">
    <!--
      Two columns on large screens with a pale full-height rule between them.
      The middle 1px grid track is the divider; lg:gap-x-16 gives it equal
      breathing room on both sides. Columns stack on small screens.
    -->
    <div
      class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-12 lg:grid-cols-[1.1fr_1px_0.9fr] lg:gap-x-16 lg:gap-y-0"
    >
      <!-- LEFT: the article (unchanged) -->
      <div>
        <!-- Eyebrow: small, uppercase, widely tracked — a quiet label above the title. -->
        <p class="mb-4 text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-zinc-500">
          Typography &middot; Sample
        </p>

        <!--
          Title practice: a heavier-but-open grade (SemiBold 600), a very tight
          line-height (leading-none) so multi-line titles read as one confident
          block, and natural letter-spacing — Lexend's letterforms are already
          open, so no condensing is needed. text-balance evens out the line
          breaks.
        -->
        <h1
          class="text-balance text-4xl font-semibold leading-none tracking-normal text-zinc-900 sm:text-5xl lg:text-6xl"
        >
          Change the way the world reads
        </h1>

        <!--
          Lede practice: larger and lighter than body, held to a shorter measure,
          set in a calm mid-gray so it steps back from the title.
        -->
        <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-zinc-600 sm:text-xl">
          A font, much like the prescription in a pair of eyeglasses, should
          change based on the reader&rsquo;s unique needs. This page is a small
          demonstration of how the Lexend typeface is meant to be set.
        </p>

        <hr class="my-12 border-zinc-200" />

        <!--
          Body practice, driven by @tailwindcss/typography:
            prose-lg              → ~18px text with ~1.78 line-height
            prose-zinc            → a fully monochrome (grayscale) prose theme
            prose-p:leading-[1.7] → generous line-height so lines never feel stacked
            prose-headings:*      → heavier, tighter section headings
            default measure (~65ch) keeps a comfortable reading line length
        -->
        <article
          class="prose prose-lg prose-zinc max-w-none
                 prose-headings:font-semibold prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-headings:text-zinc-900
                 prose-p:leading-[1.7] prose-p:text-zinc-700
                 prose-li:text-zinc-700
                 prose-a:font-medium prose-a:text-zinc-900 prose-a:underline prose-a:decoration-zinc-300 prose-a:underline-offset-2 hover:prose-a:decoration-zinc-900
                 prose-strong:text-zinc-900
                 prose-blockquote:border-l-zinc-300 prose-blockquote:font-normal prose-blockquote:not-italic prose-blockquote:text-zinc-600"
        >
          <h2>Three ideas behind the letters</h2>
          <p>
            Lexend was designed around reading proficiency rather than pure
            style. Three typographic decisions do the work:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>A sans-serif face</strong> to reduce cognitive noise, so the
              eye spends less effort decoding each shape.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Expanded scaling</strong> that improves the potential for character
              recognition.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Hyper-expanded character spacing</strong>, which creates a little
              more lag time between glyphs and reduces crowding.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Setting titles</h2>
          <p>
            Headlines carry weight &mdash; literally. Give them a heavier grade
            (600&ndash;700), pull the line-height in close so multi-line titles
            hold together, and let the tracking stay tight. Because
            Lexend&rsquo;s letterforms are already open, titles read as confident
            without feeling cramped. Keep the measure short; a title rarely needs
            the full width of the column.
          </p>

          <h2>Setting body text</h2>
          <p>
            Body copy is where Lexend earns its keep. In the original readability
            study, all text was set at 16&nbsp;pt &mdash; treat that as a floor,
            not a ceiling. This page runs slightly larger for comfort on screens.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three habits keep paragraphs calm: a generous line-height (around
            1.7&ndash;1.8) so lines never feel stacked; a comfortable measure of
            roughly 60&ndash;75 characters so the eye reliably finds the next
            line; and a normal weight (400) that lets the expanded spacing
            breathe. Resist the urge to tighten &mdash; crowding is the exact
            thing the typeface was built to avoid.
          </p>

          <h2>One color, on purpose</h2>
          <p>
            The palette here is deliberately monochrome. Stripping color down to
            a single grayscale ramp puts all the emphasis on rhythm, contrast,
            and spacing &mdash; the things type is actually made of. Text sits
            near-black on near-white, secondary notes step back into mid-gray,
            and rules stay quiet. It is a starting point you can add an accent
            color to later without touching the type.
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            Good typography is invisible: you notice the reading, not the letters.
          </blockquote>
        </article>
      </div>

      <!-- MIDDLE: pale vertical divider (large screens only) -->
      <div class="hidden bg-zinc-200 lg:block" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <!-- RIGHT: the type specimen -->
      <Specimen />
    </div>

    <footer class="mt-16 border-t border-zinc-200 pt-8 text-sm text-zinc-500">
      Built with Astro, Tailwind CSS, and Lexend. Typography practices adapted
      from <a
        href="https://www.lexend.com"
        class="underline decoration-zinc-300 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-zinc-900"
        >lexend.com</a
      >.
    </footer>
  </main>
</Layout>
